                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Detailed Breakdown: Speed & Complexity',
                    'content' => '<h3>1. Zero CMS</h3><p><strong>Speed:</strong> Instantaneous (&lt;1.5ms total request turnaround). By bypassing Composer autoload cascades and utilising a single-query UNION handshake for bootstrapping, Zero reads and executes in a single process step.</p><p><strong>Complexity:</strong> Zero. It is fully self-contained in a few small files with no external vendor directories. This guarantees that your application will never break due to a deprecated package or security patch in an upstream library.</p><h3>2. WordPress</h3><p><strong>Speed:</strong> Slow (150ms+ bare boot time). WordPress suffers from decades of backward-compatibility legacy code. Every request loops over thousands of global actions, hooks, and database option queries.</p><p><strong>Complexity:</strong> High legacy burden. The codebase mixes procedural code, object-oriented layers, and global variables, making enterprise extensibility fragile and highly vulnerable to third-party plugin exploits.</p><h3>3. Laravel</h3><p><strong>Speed:</strong> Moderate (40ms+ bare boot time). On startup, Laravel must boot dozens of service providers, instantiate container bindings, compile configuration directories, and load extensive Eloquent relations. Under load, this container bootstrap consumes major CPU cycles.</p><p><strong>Complexity:</strong> Extremely High. The <code>vendor/</code> directory in a standard Laravel project contains tens of thousands of files across dozens of nested packages. Updates often trigger cascade breakages and package dependency conflicts.</p><h3>4. SilverStripe</h3><p><strong>Speed:</strong> Moderate to Slow (80ms+ boot time). SilverStripe relies on deep configuration compilation (YAML), extensive template caching, and a heavy DataObject ORM mapped dynamically via reflection.</p><p><strong>Complexity:</strong> High. Developers must learn custom YAML schemas, custom template languages (SSViewer), and manage heavy database schemas that require constant support with each dynamic framework iteration.</p>',
