        [
            'title' => 'Understanding Time-Ordered UUIDv7 Keys',
            'slug' => 'docs/how-tos/uuidv7',
            'status' => 'published',
            'site_domain' => 'd6laptop.zero.guide',
            'content' => [
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Why UUIDv7? The Database Performance Crisis',
                    'content' => '<p>Standard databases frequently rely on auto-incrementing integer keys (e.g. <code>1, 2, 3...</code>) or random UUIDv4 string hashes. However, both of these introduce major drawbacks in multi-tenant enterprise architectures:</p><ul><li><strong>Integer leaking</strong>: Sequential keys allow attackers to guess total records volume easily.</li><li><strong>Page Splits Fragmentation</strong>: Random UUIDv4 inserts occur randomly across the database B-Tree index, causing severe disk page splitting, high disk I/O, and indexing fragmentation under InnoDB.</li></ul>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Anatomy of a Time-Ordered UUIDv7 Key',
                    'content' => '<p>UUIDv7 (defined in RFC 9562) solves this natively. It is a 128-bit string structured sequentially with a timestamp prefix:</p><pre><code> 019ec4ee-fdda-7049-81d9-446133464390
 └───┬───┘ └──┬─┘ └──┬─┘ └──────┬─────┘
  48-bit     4-bit  12-bit   64-bit Random
 Timestamp    Ver  Variant     Entropy Bits</code></pre><ul><li><strong>First 48 bits</strong>: Millisecond Unix epoch timestamp (ensures time-ordered sorting).</li><li><strong>Entropy bits</strong>: Provides cryptographic randomness to guarantee absolute uniqueness across replication nodes.</li></ul>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Lexicographical Sort and B-Tree Insertion',
                    'content' => '<p>Because the prefix is time-sequential, newly generated UUIDv7 keys are always appended at the <strong>very end</strong> of the InnoDB B-Tree index. This transforms random writes into fast sequential appends, matching integer-like speed while retaining standard UUID uniqueness!</p>',
                ],
            ],
            'type' => 'page',
            'show_in_nav' => '0',
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'SMTP TCP Socket Emailing',
