                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Zero CMS Security Philosophy',
                    'content' => '<p>Security in Zero CMS is baked directly into the core kernel class handlers. By rejecting massive, third-party libraries, we eliminate the primary attack vector of modern PHP applications (upstream package exploits). Every protection pipeline—including CSRF token validation, input sanitization, HTML scrubbers, and spam traps—is written natively, keeping the system extremely fast, transparent, and secure.</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '1. CSRF Protection (Security::csrfVerify)',
                    'content' => '<p>To protect against Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks, Zero CMS automatically generates a secure, random state token for each session. Form submissions must embed this token. State-changing HTTP request routes (POST, PUT, DELETE) are intercepted by <code>CsrfMiddleware</code>, which calls <code>Security::csrfVerify()</code>. If the token is invalid or missing, the request is dropped immediately before any database writes are triggered.</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'code',
                    'title' => 'CSRF Form Generation & Verification',
                    'language' => 'php',
                    'code' => '// 1. Form view template helper generates the input
echo \\Zero\\Support\\Security::csrfInput();
// Output: <input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="a1f9e8...">

// 2. Middleware validates token before processing inputs
if ($_SERVER[\'REQUEST_METHOD\'] === \'POST\') {
    $token = $_POST[\'csrf\'] ?? \'\';
    if (!\\Zero\\Support\\Security::csrfVerify($token)) {
        header(\'HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden\');
        die(\'Invalid CSRF handshake token.\');
    }
}',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '2. Input Sanitization & HTML DOM Scrubbing',
                    'content' => '<p>Zero CMS employs double-walled defenses against Cross-Site Scripting (XSS). Incoming scalar values are recursively sanitized by <code>Security::sanitizeInput()</code> to strip standard HTML tags and null bytes. Rich text data (which must support HTML layouts) is scrubbed using <code>Security::sanitizeHtml()</code>. This sanitizer uses PHP\'s native <code>DOMDocument</code> and <code>DOMXPath</code> to parse the HTML string, completely removing blacklisted tags (<code>&lt;script&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;embed&gt;</code>, etc.) and dangerous event attributes (e.g. <code>onclick</code>, <code>onload</code>) natively.</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'code',
                    'title' => 'Global HTML Sanitizer (Security.php)',
                    'language' => 'php',
                    'code' => 'namespace Zero\\Support;

class Security {
    public static function sanitizeHtml(string $html): string {
        // Uses native DOMDocument and DOMXPath to purge scripts and event listeners...
        $doc = new \\DOMDocument();
        $doc->loadHTML(\'<?xml encoding="utf-8" ?>\' . $html, LIBXML_HTML_NOIMPLIED | LIBXML_HTML_NODEFDTD);
        // Strips script, iframe, meta, form, style, and strips attributes with \'javascript:\' or \'on*\' ...
        return trim($doc->saveHTML());
    }
}',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '3. Stealth Honeypot Spam Prevention',
                    'content' => '<p>Dynamic forms on our sites use zero-friction stealth honeypots to block automated spam bots. A hidden input named <code>website_url</code> is embedded inside the form. Bots are highly primed to fill this field. To prevent bot detection, the word <strong>\'honeypot\'</strong> never appears in the class names, inputs, or styles. Instead, a standard-looking wrapper class (<code>.website-field-wrapper</code>) is styled with <code>display: none; visibility: hidden;</code> on CSS sheets to hide the field from human eyes. If the bait field is populated upon submission, the request is silently dropped, returning a simulated success response to the bot without executing any database writes or dispatching emails.</p>',
                ],
            ],
            'type' => 'page',
            'show_in_nav' => '0',
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Custom Column Rendering (listView)',
            'slug' => 'docs/how-tos/custom-views',
            'status' => 'published',
            'site_domain' => 'd6laptop.zero.guide',
            'content' => [
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Introduction',
                    'content' => '<p>Zero CMS provides an elegant mechanism to customize column output in admin listings without hacking the controller or list layout views. By defining a <code>listView</code> parameter in the model\'s <code>getConfig()</code> array, you can point a model column to a specific PHP view file.</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'How it Works Behind the Scenes',
                    'content' => '<p>Template::renderFile($viewPath, [
    \'value\' => $record-&gt;{$field},
    \'record\' => $record
]);</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Example 1: Color-Coded Status Badges',
                    'content' => '<p>To render the <code>status</code> column of a Model as color-coded status badges, define the column with <code>listView</code> in your model configuration:</p><pre><code class="language-php">// Inside src/Models/Page.php
\'status\' => [
    \'type\' => \'select\',
    \'label\' => \'Status\',
    \'editable\' => true,
    \'listDisplay\' => true,
    \'listView\' => \'fields/status\',
    \'required\' => true
]</code></pre><p>Then, create the view at <code>src/Modules/Admin/Views/fields/status.php</code>:</p><pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php
$val = strtolower($value ?? \'\');
$icon = \'status-draft\';
$class = \'status-draft\';

if ($val === \'published\' || $val === \'completed\') {
    $icon = \'status-published\';
    $class = \'status-published\';
} elseif ($val === \'archived\') {
    $icon = \'status-archived\';
    $class = \'status-archived\';
}
?&gt;
&lt;span class="status-badge &lt;?php echo $class; ?&gt;"&gt;
    &lt;span class="icon-svg" title="&lt;?php echo htmlspecialchars($val); ?&gt;"&gt;
        &lt;?php echo \\Zero\\Core\\App::svg($icon); ?&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;</code></pre>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Example 2: Site Enabled Modules as Pills',
                    'content' => '<p>To display a site\'s enabled modules as color-coded pills instead of raw JSON text, specify <code>\'listView\' => \'fields/modules\'</code> in your Site model config:</p><pre><code class="language-php">// Inside src/Models/Site.php
\'enabled_modules\' => [
    \'type\' => \'modules\',
    \'label\' => \'Enabled Modules\',
    \'editable\' => true,
    \'listDisplay\' => true,
    \'listView\' => \'fields/modules\'
]</code></pre><p>Then implement the view in <code>src/Modules/Admin/Views/fields/modules.php</code>. It decodes the JSON array of modules and outputs them as a series of pills styled with CSS:</p><pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php
$modules = json_decode($value ?? \'[]\', true);
if (!is_array($modules)) {
    $modules = [];
}
?&gt;
&lt;div class="module-pills-container"&gt;
    &lt;?php if (empty($modules)): ?&gt;
        &lt;span class="module-pill module-empty"&gt;No modules&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;?php else: ?&gt;
        &lt;?php foreach ($modules as $module): ?&gt;
            &lt;?php
            $moduleLower = strtolower($module);
            $icon = \'settings\';
            $label = htmlspecialchars($module);
            
            if ($moduleLower === \'blog\') {
                $icon = \'edit-3\';
                $label = \'Blog\';
            } elseif ($moduleLower === \'shop\') {
                $icon = \'shop\';
                $label = \'Shop\';
            } elseif ($moduleLower === \'howtos\') {
                $icon = \'book-open\';
                $label = \'How-Tos\';
            }
            ?&gt;
            &lt;span class="module-pill module-&lt;?php echo htmlspecialchars($moduleLower); ?&gt;" title="&lt;?php echo $label; ?&gt;"&gt;
                &lt;span class="icon-svg"&gt;&lt;?php echo \\Zero\\Core\\App::svg($icon); ?&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="module-label"&gt;&lt;?php echo $label; ?&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;?php endforeach; ?&gt;
    &lt;?php endif; ?&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;',
