        [
            'title' => 'Background Jobs & Task Scheduler Module',
            'slug' => 'docs/modules/jobs',
            'status' => 'published',
            'show_in_nav' => '0',
            'site_domain' => 'd6laptop.zero.guide',
            'content' => [
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '1. High-Level Architecture',
                    'content' => '<p>The Zero CMS Job Queue Module provides a robust, zero-dependency, lightweight background task manager. Unlike heavy enterprise frameworks (such as Laravel) that rely on complex object serialization and continuous memory-hogging daemons, Zero CMS uses stateless JSON-based job payloads and transactional double-locking to ensure maximum concurrency speed, security, and absolute tenant isolation.</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '2. Database Column Schema',
                    'content' => '<p>Background tasks are persisted inside the <code>queue_jobs</code> database table. This table tracks the fully qualified class name (FQN) of the job handler, serialized parameters, current execution attempts, locks, failures, and traceback logs:</p><pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE queue_jobs (
    id VARCHAR(36) PRIMARY KEY,
    site_id VARCHAR(36) NOT NULL,
    job_class VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    payload JSON NOT NULL,
    status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT \'pending\',
    attempts INT DEFAULT 0,
    reserved_at DATETIME NULL,
    failed_at DATETIME NULL,
    error_message TEXT NULL
);</code></pre>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '3. Concurrency Race & Deadlock Prevention',
                    'content' => '<p>To guarantee that two parallel worker threads never process the same background job concurrently, the Queue Runner implements a <strong>double-locking race safety protocol</strong> inside a strict database transaction:</p><ol><li><strong>Row-Locking Selection</strong>: Uses <code>FOR UPDATE</code> to lock the oldest pending job row: <pre><code class="language-sql">SELECT id FROM queue_jobs WHERE status = \'pending\' LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE;</code></pre></li><li><strong>State Transition Update</strong>: Immediately updates the row status to <code>\'reserved\'</code> and increments attempts before committing: <pre><code class="language-sql">UPDATE queue_jobs SET status = \'reserved\' WHERE id = ?;</code></pre></li><li><strong>Commit Lock Release</strong>: Commits the transaction to release the row lock for other workers while keeping the job marked as reserved.</li></ol>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '4. Dual Execution Runners',
                    'content' => '<p>To support all hosting environments, the module features two execution models:</p><ul><li><strong>CLI Daemon Worker</strong>: A lightweight console command loop that polls and processes the queue via background terminal threads (ideal for VPS/dedicated servers).</li><li><strong>Async Web Webhook Trigger</strong>: A fire-and-forget fallback that triggers the queue processor using raw socket handshakes (<code>fsockopen</code>) dynamically during standard client web requests, avoiding any client-side execution delay (ideal for shared hosting without cron access).</li></ul>',
                ],
            ],
            'type' => 'page',
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Creating and Dispatching Queue Jobs',
            'slug' => 'docs/modules/jobs/tutorials',
            'status' => 'published',
            'show_in_nav' => '0',
            'site_domain' => 'd6laptop.zero.guide',
            'content' => [
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Introduction',
                    'content' => '<p>This guide explains how to create, register, and dispatch custom background jobs inside the Zero CMS Job Queue system. It walks through building a stateless job, queueing it from code, and running the processing pipeline.</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Step 1: Build a Stateless Job Class',
                    'content' => '<p>Every background job in the system must implement the <code>Zero\\Interfaces\\Job</code> interface. To maintain loose coupling, place your jobs inside your module\'s <code>Jobs/</code> directory. Keep the job class stateless; all dynamic data should be passed via a primitive parameters payload:</p><pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

namespace Zero\\Modules\\Shop\\Jobs;

use Exception;
use Zero\Core\App;
use Zero\Core\Env;
use Zero\Database\DB;
use Zero\Interfaces\BlockHelperInterface;
use Zero\Interfaces\Controller;
use Zero\Interfaces\Job as JobInterface;
use Zero\Interfaces\Model;
use Zero\Interfaces\SeederInterface;
use Zero\Models\Traits\HasSlug;
use Zero\Models\Traits\IsModel;
use Zero\Modules\Blog\Models\Post;
use Zero\Modules\Search\Traits\Searchable;
use Zero\Modules\Shop\Jobs\SendOrderReceipt;
use Zero\Support\Emailer;
use Zero\Support\Security;

class SendOrderReceipt implements JobInterface
{
    public function execute(array $payload): void
    {
        $orderId = $payload[\'order_id\'];
        $email = $payload[\'email\'];
        
        // Execute raw SMTP Socket mailer
        Emailer::send($email, "Order Receipt #{$orderId}", "Thank you for your purchase!");
    }
}</code></pre>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Step 2: Dispatch the Job to the Queue',
                    'content' => '<p>To queue your task, invoke the static <code>QueueManager::dispatch()</code> helper from anywhere in your application (controllers, hooks, model events, etc.). The dispatcher automatically serializes parameters to JSON and generates a unique UUIDv7 tracking ID:</p><pre><code class="language-php">use Zero\\Modules\\Queue\\Support\\QueueManager;
// Queue receipt dispatching
$jobId = QueueManager::dispatch(
    jobClass: SendOrderReceipt::class,
    payload: [
        \'order_id\' => $order-&gt;id,
        \'email\' => $order-&gt;customer_email
    ]
);</code></pre>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Step 3: Process the Job Queue',
                    'content' => '<p>To run the background processor, you can use either the console runner or trigger the API endpoint:</p><ul><li><strong>Console Command</strong>: Execute the processing script sequentially: <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/queue-runner</code></pre></li><li><strong>Web Endpoint Callback</strong>: Make an HTTP POST call to the webhook processor callback endpoint: <pre><code class="language-bash">curl -X POST http://mysite.zero/api/v1/queue/process</code></pre></li></ul>',
                ],
            ],
