            'title' => 'Configuring Google Cloud Storage (Zero Dependencies)',
            'slug' => 'docs/how-tos/gcs-setup',
            'status' => 'published',
            'show_in_nav' => '0',
            'site_domain' => 'd6laptop.zero.guide',
            'content' => [
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Introduction',
                    'content' => '<p>Zero CMS features a highly optimized, fully integrated, and 100% zero-dependency Google Cloud Storage (GCS) driver. This custom driver bypasses heavy external SDKs entirely, executing secure OAuth 2.0 JWT exchanges via native PHP OpenSSL and file uploads via raw PHP cURL sessions directly for maximum execution speed, footprint reduction, and supply-chain protection.</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Step 1: Obtain Google Service Account Key JSON',
                    'content' => '<p>To authorize REST API requests with Google, you must obtain a Service Account Key file from your Google Cloud Console:</p><ol><li>Navigate to **IAM & Admin > Service Accounts** in the GCP Console.</li><li>Create a service account and assign it the **Storage Admin** role (or full storage control).</li><li>Click on the newly created service account, navigate to the **Keys** tab, and select **Add Key > Create New Key**.</li><li>Choose the **JSON** format, click **Create**, and save the downloaded file securely onto your server disk (e.g. at <code>/data/misc/zero/etc/gcp-key.json</code>, keeping it outside the public web root).</li></ol>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Step 2: Configure Environment Variables (.env)',
                    'content' => '<p>Open your local environmental configuration file (<code>.env</code>) and update your storage credentials. Our zero-dependency driver supports both GCP bucket access models seamlessly:</p><ul><li><strong>Uniform Bucket-Level Access (Recommended)</strong>: Keeps security centrally managed at the bucket level. To make files public, grant the <strong>Storage Object Viewer</strong> (<code>roles/storage.objectViewer</code>) role to the member <strong><code>allUsers</code></strong> centrally in your GCP Console, and leave <code>GCS_PREDEFINED_ACL</code> empty or commented out inside your <code>.env</code>.</li><li><strong>Fine-Grained (ACL-based) Access</strong>: Manage access per file. Turn Uniform access OFF centrally in GCP, and define <code>GCS_PREDEFINED_ACL=publicRead</code> inside your <code>.env</code>. Our custom driver will automatically append public-read permissions to every uploaded asset dynamically.</li></ul><pre><code class="language-ini"># Set active storage driver to GCS
STORAGE_DRIVER=gcs

# Define target Google Cloud Bucket name
GCS_BUCKET_NAME=your_gcs_bucket_name_here

# Define Google Cloud Project ID
GCS_PROJECT_ID=your_gcp_project_id_here

# Define the absolute physical path to your service account key file
GCS_KEY_FILE=/data/misc/zero/etc/gcp-key.json

# Optional: Define predefined ACL (use \'publicRead\' for Fine-Grained buckets, leave empty for Uniform buckets)
GCS_PREDEFINED_ACL=publicRead</code></pre>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => 'Step 3: Utilizing the Storage API',
                    'content' => '<p>Once configured, the core CMS will automatically route all file storage, media uploads, and crops through your zero-dependency GCS driver. You can also invoke the static <code>Storage</code> API helper from anywhere in your custom modules:</p><pre><code class="language-php">use Zero\\Core\\Storage\\Storage;

// 1. Upload a file
Storage::putFile(\'uploads/avatars/user-123.jpg\', \'/tmp/avatar.jpg\');

// 2. Check if file exists in the cloud bucket
if (Storage::exists(\'uploads/avatars/user-123.jpg\')) {
    // 3. Resolve the public GCS public URL
    $url = Storage::getUrl(\'uploads/avatars/user-123.jpg\');
    // Returns: https://storage.googleapis.com/your-bucket/uploads/avatars/user-123.jpg
}</code></pre>',
                ],
            ],
            'type' => 'page',
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Secure Frontend Uploads & Private Storage',
            'slug' => 'docs/how-tos/secure-uploads',
            'status' => 'published',
            'show_in_nav' => '0',
            'site_domain' => 'd6laptop.zero.guide',
            'content' => [
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '1. High-Level Architectural Summary',
                    'content' => '<p>Zero CMS features a highly secure, unified public/private media storage architecture. This system enables developers to support secure file uploads from frontend forms (such as resumes, CVs, confidential agreements) that are public-uploadable but restricted strictly to logged-in back-office administrators. By combining secure metadata fields directly into the unified <code>media</code> table and isolating files inside a protective private storage directory, Zero CMS completely eliminates directory crawling, direct URL-guessing, and malicious script execution (backdoors) exploits.</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '2. Configuration and Storage Setup',
                    'content' => '<p>To support secure private uploads locally, ensure your server has a protected private directory on disk that cannot be accessed directly via HTTP requests:</p><ul><li><strong>Local Storage Directory</strong>: Create the directory <code>/data/misc/zero/storage/private/</code> on your disk, applying defensive group permissions (<code>chmod 0775</code>, owned by <code>www-data</code>).</li><li><strong>Access Control htaccess</strong>: Embed a protective <code>.htaccess</code> file inside the private folder containing <code>Deny from all</code> to block Apache/Nginx from direct web-serves: <pre><code class="language-text"># /data/misc/zero/storage/private/.htaccess
Deny from all</code></pre></li><li><strong>Cloud Storage Buckets</strong>: If using GCS or S3, ensure that public access (e.g. the <code>allUsers</code> Storage Object Viewer role) is strictly disabled on your private directories or private buckets.</li></ul>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '3. Extension Developer Guide: Uploading Private Files',
                    'content' => '<p>To implement private attachments inside your extension controllers or forms, execute the following three secure upload steps:</p><ol><li><strong>Obfuscate Filename</strong>: Rename the user-supplied filename to a cryptographically secure UUIDv7 string. This prevents attackers from guessing the file\'s location.</li><li><strong>Save to Private Storage</strong>: Move the temporary uploaded file to your private directory.</li><li><strong>Persist Unified Media Record</strong>: Create a new <code>Media</code> active record, setting the <code>visibility</code> parameter to <code>\'private\'</code>:</li></ol><pre><code class="language-php">use Zero\\Models\\Media;

// 1. Move temporary file to secure private storage folder
$uuid = \\Zero\\Support\\Security::uuidv7();
$diskName = "{$uuid}.bin";
$privatePath = "/storage/private/{$diskName}";

move_uploaded_file($_FILES[\'cv_file\'][\'tmp_name\'], APPLICATION_ROOT . $privatePath);

// 2. Persist metadata as private Media record
$media = new Media([
    \'filename\' => $diskName,
    \'path\' => $privatePath,
    \'mime\' => $_FILES[\'cv_file\'][\'type\'],
    \'title\' => \'Candidate CV - \' . $_POST[\'candidate_name\'],
    \'visibility\' => \'private\',               // Strict Gated Visibility!
    \'submission_id\' => $submissionId,        // Link to Form Submission
    \'original_name\' => $_FILES[\'cv_file\'][\'name\'],
    \'file_size\' => $_FILES[\'cv_file\'][\'size\']
]);
$media->save();</code></pre>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '4. Extension Developer Guide: Secure Retrievals & Gated Streams',
                    'content' => '<p>Once saved as private, the Media model automatically handles secure gated routing. Calling the public helper <code>$media->getUrl()</code> will intercept and return the secure download route <code>/admin/secure-download/{id}</code> instead of the physical path:</p><pre><code class="language-php">// Unified Auto-Routing Helper
$url = $media->getUrl(); 
// Returns: /admin/secure-download/019efb8-2280-7904-9b2c-8463500ac881</code></pre><p>When an administrator clicks the link, the core <code>SecureDownloadController</code> intercepts the request, enforces administrative session and roles authentication, verifies multi-tenant boundaries (ensuring Site A editor cannot download Site B files), and streams the original file bytes directly to the browser with protective, non-cacheable headers:</p><pre><code class="language-php"># Secure Gatekeeper Stream Headers
header(\'Content-Type: \' . $file[\'mime_type\']);
header(\'Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="\' . basename($file[\'original_name\']) . \'"\');
header(\'Cache-Control: private, must-revalidate\');</code></pre>',
                ],
            ],
            'type' => 'page',
        ],
