        [
            'title' => 'Search Index & Decoupled Driver Architecture',
            'slug' => 'docs/how-tos/search-architecture',
            'status' => 'published',
            'show_in_nav' => '0',
            'site_domain' => 'd6laptop.zero.guide',
            'content' => [
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '1. High-Level Architectural Summary',
                    'content' => '<p>Zero CMS features an advanced, 100% decoupled **Search Index and Driver Architecture**. Moving away from slow, un-indexed cross-table dynamic lookups, the system compiles searchable content into a dedicated <code>search_index</code> database table in real-time. This index-based search guarantees ultra-fast query execution, scales gracefully under heavy traffic, enforces strict multi-tenant boundary scoping, and supports weighted **Title-Hit Boosting** to ensure that relevance ranking prioritizes exact and partial title matches.</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '2. Decoupled Search Drivers',
                    'content' => '<p>By utilizing a driver interface model, the search core is fully decoupled from the underlying index storage. This allows administrators to hot-swap search drivers based on environment variables (e.g., swapping from standard MySQL matching to Elasticsearch or Algolia) without modifying any application controller code.</p><pre><code class="language-ini"># Configure active search index driver (defaults to database)
SEARCH_DRIVER=database</code></pre>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '3. The Hybrid Block Helper Model (No N+1 Queries)',
                    'content' => '<p>A critical architectural mandate of Zero CMS is the **absolute prevention of N+1 database queries**. When extracting search text from page-builder layouts (which are saved as structured JSON arrays of blocks), instantiating full layout renderers or calling individual database hooks for block assets would trigger a catastrophic N+1 query loop. To prevent this, Zero CMS implements a **Hybrid Block Helper Model**:</p><ul><li><strong>OOP Block Helpers:</strong> Blocks implement a standard <code>BlockHelperInterface</code>, acting as lightweight, in-memory data-transfer objects that extract searchable plain text from JSON attributes with <strong>zero database queries</strong>.</li><li><strong>Recursive Fallback Scanner:</strong> If a custom block does not have a specialized helper class defined, the indexing engine automatically falls back to an intelligent recursive array scanner, keeping the search index 100% self-healing and backward-compatible.</li></ul>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'code',
                    'title' => '4. Developer How-To: Implementing Searchable Models',
                    'language' => 'php',
                    'code' => 'namespace Zero\\Custom\\Models;

class CustomBook implements Model
{
    // 1. Import Searchable Trait alongside standard active-record IsModel
    use IsModel, Searchable;

    protected static $tableName = \'custom_books\';
    protected static $modelType = \'book\';

    // 2. Include \'exclude_from_search\' in DB and fillables
    protected static $fillable = [\'title\', \'content\', \'status\', \'exclude_from_search\'];

    public $exclude_from_search = 0;
    public $status;
}',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'code',
                    'title' => '5. Developer How-To: Creating Custom Block Helpers',
                    'language' => 'php',
                    'code' => 'namespace Zero\\Blocks;

class ProductShowcaseBlock implements BlockHelperInterface
{
    protected array $data;

    public function __construct(array $data)
    {
        $this->data = $data;
    }

    /**
     * Compile plain text content from block parameters with 0 database hits.
     */
    public function getSearchableContent(): string
    {
        $parts = [];
        if (!empty($this->data[\'heading\'])) {
            $parts[] = $this->data[\'heading\'];
        }
        if (!empty($this->data[\'product_name\'])) {
            $parts[] = $this->data[\'product_name\'];
        }
        if (!empty($this->data[\'description\'])) {
            $parts[] = strip_tags($this->data[\'description\']);
        }
        return implode(\' \', $parts);
    }
}',
                ],
            ],
            'type' => 'page',
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Serverless Blueprints: Google Cloud Run & Cloud SQL Setup',
            'slug' => 'docs/how-tos/deploy-cloud-run',
            'status' => 'published',
            'show_in_nav' => '0',
            'site_domain' => 'd6laptop.zero.guide',
            'content' => [
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '1. High-Level Architectural Summary',
                    'content' => '<p>Zero CMS features a highly optimized, fully stateless backend perfectly suited for modern serverless container environments like <strong>Google Cloud Run</strong>. By utilizing lightweight native PHP and bypassing heavy third-party core packages, the bootstrap cycle of Zero CMS executes in under <strong>1.5 milliseconds</strong>. This raw execution speed completely eliminates serverless latency cold-starts: container scaling is immediate and entirely transparent to end-users.</p><p>In a production serverless environment, the CMS operates in a fully decoupled architecture:</p><ul><li><strong>Stateless Compute:</strong> Google Cloud Run manages stateless PHP containers, scaling down to <code>0</code> active instances when idle to achieve absolute base-cost optimization.</li><li><strong>Relational Storage:</strong> A Google Cloud SQL MySQL database instance manages data tables with strict multi-tenant boundaries.</li><li><strong>Object Storage:</strong> Asset uploads and cropped media files stream directly to a Google Cloud Storage bucket (or AWS S3) via zero-dependency drivers.</li></ul>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '2. Sizing Strategy for the Lowest-Cost Setup',
                    'content' => '<p>To host Zero CMS with maximum performance for the lowest possible price (under $10/month or even fully covered under GCP\'s Free Tier under low traffic), configure your Google Cloud components with these precise settings:</p><ul><li><strong>Cloud Run Compute Sizing:</strong> Allocate <code>512 MB RAM</code> and <code>1 CPU</code> per container instance. Set the <em>Minimum Instances</em> to <code>0</code> (so that you pay exactly $0.00 when there is no traffic) and set the CPU Allocation to <strong>"CPU is only allocated during request processing"</strong> to avoid paying for idle server cycles.</li><li><strong>Cloud SQL Database Sizing:</strong> Provision a shared-core <code>db-f1-micro</code> database instance (the smallest tier available, which costs around $9/month) running MySQL 8.0. Set the storage disk type to a 10 GB HDD (or SSD) and disable "automatic storage increase" for development to lock in the baseline rate. Additionally, use <strong>Single-Zone</strong> hosting to bypass multi-zone replication costs.</li><li><strong>Cloud Storage Sizing:</strong> Standard GCS Buckets cost only $0.02 per GB per month. Enable GCS lifecycle policies to auto-purge temporary files after 30 days to optimize storage overhead.</li></ul>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'text',
                    'title' => '3. Provisioning Cloud Resources (Cloud SQL & GCS)',
                    'content' => '<p>To provision the GCP infrastructure natively using the Google Cloud CLI, execute the following commands in your terminal. Ensure that you have already run <code>gcloud auth login</code> and set your target GCP project via <code>gcloud config set project YOUR_PROJECT_ID</code>.</p><p><strong>Step 1. Provision GCS Storage Bucket:</strong> Create an optimized, uniform-access Google Cloud Storage bucket to host public media uploads and cached crops:</p>',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'code',
                    'title' => 'Create Google Cloud Storage Bucket',
                    'language' => 'bash',
                    'code' => 'gcloud storage buckets create gs://YOUR_BUCKET_NAME --location=YOUR_REGION --uniform-bucket-level-access',
                ],
                [
